tor her blood pressures at home.\n\nDiabetes type 2.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has noticed intermittent elevations of her blood glucose levels but has been compliant with the use of metformin. Her diet has been limited due to nausea in the setting of Lyme disease.\n• Additional Testing: I am going to order a hemoglobin A1c to ensure no medication changes are needed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, how are you doing?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay. i\'ve been feeling pretty tired.\n[doctor] okay. how long have you been feeling tired?\n[patient] um, a couple of days now.\n[doctor] okay. and have you noticed anything else? any other symptoms?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve had a lot of body aches. and i have a rash on my leg. and my elbows are really swollen.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had a fever?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t had a fever.\n[doctor] okay. and what\'s the rash look like?\n[patient] it\'s like a bullseye.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been able to eat?\n[patient] not really. i haven\'t been able to eat much.\n[doctor] okay. what have you been able to eat?\n[patient] just crackers. bland foods.\n[doctor] okay. and you mentioned swollen elbows. is that bilateral or just one side?\n[patient] both.\n[doctor] okay. and you said you\'ve been feeling tired and body aches. have you noticed anything else?\n[patient] no, that\'s about it.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m gon na go ahead and review your chart here. you\'ve had a lyme titer drawn. and it came back positive. have you been out hiking or anything?\n[patient] yeah, i was hiking in the woods last weekend.\n[doctor] okay. and did you notice the tick bite?\n[patient] i didn\'t notice it at the time, but i did notice it later.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been feeling well otherwise?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been okay.\n[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any abdominal pain or diarrhea?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and how about your blood sugars? how have they been?\n[patient] they\'ve been okay. sometimes a little high or low, but nothing too far out of range.\n[doctor] okay. and are you still taking metformin?\n[patient] yes, i am.\n[doctor] okay. and how about your blood pressure? how have you been doing?\n[patient] my blood pressure\'s been good. i\'ve been monitoring it at home.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been avoiding salty foods?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been trying to avoid salty foods.\n[doctor] okay. and are you still taking lisinopril?\n[patient] yes, i am.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m gon na go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i\'m gon na start with your abdomen. does this hurt at all? (examining the abdomen) okay. and then i\'m gon na move on to your joints. (examining the joints) okay. and then i\'m gon na look at your rash. (examining the rash) okay. so i see a small bullseye rash on your left abdominal quadrant. i\'m gon na go ahead and review your labs here. your white blood cell count is within normal limits. all of your other labs are normal. i\'m gon na go ahead and order a lipid panel just to check your cholesterol levels. for your lyme disease, i\'m gon na prescribe doxycycline 100 milligrams twice a day for three weeks. i\'m gon na touch base with an infectious disease doctor to make sure we don\'t need to do anything else. i want to see you again in a week for follow-up for this. for your hypertension, you\'ve been doing well. you\'ve been monitoring your blood pressure at home, avoiding salty foods, and taking your lisinopril. i\'m gon na continue that. i\'m gon na refill that for you today. for your type 2 diabetes, your blood sugars have been okay. you\'ve noticed intermittent elevations, but you\'ve been compliant with metformin. your diet has been limited due to nausea in the setting of lyme disease. i\'m gon na order a hemoglobin a1c to ensure we don\'t need to make any changes. does that sound good to you?\n[patient] yeah, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any questions?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. all right. i\'ll see you in a week.\n[patient] thank you.\n[doctor] you\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay. i\'m just really tired and feeling a little bit nauseous.\n[doctor] okay. so you said you\'re feeling tired and nauseous. do you know why?\n[patient] i think it\'s because i got bitten by an insect.\n[doctor] okay. do you know what kind of insect?\n[patient] i think it was a tick.\n[doctor] okay. did you get it removed?\n[patient] i did. i did. it was it was on my leg. i just got it removed yesterday.\n[doctor] okay. and you said you were feeling tired and nauseous. do you have any other symptoms?\n[patient] um, yeah, i have a little bit of a rash.\n[doctor] okay. where is it?\n[patient] it\'s on my left side.\n[doctor] okay. and is it a rash or a bump?\n[patient] it\'s a rash.\n[doctor] okay. and it\'s on your left side of your body?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and how long has this rash been there?\n[patient] it just started a few days ago.\n[doctor] okay. and you said you were feeling tired and nauseous. do you have any other symptoms?\n[patient] i do. i have a lot of body aches. my elbows are really swollen and sore.\n[doctor] okay. and you said you have a rash on your left side. is it on your elbow?\n[patient] no, it\'s on my stomach.\n[doctor] okay. and you said your elbow is swollen and sore. do you have any other joint pain?\n[patient] yeah, my knees are sore too.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any fevers?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t had a fever.\n[doctor] okay. and how are you eating?\n[patient] i haven\'t been eating much.\n[doctor] okay. what kind of food are you eating?\n[patient] i\'ve been eating crackers. i haven\'t been able to eat much.\n[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and you said you have diabetes. how are you doing with 