-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up adult hydrocephalus, 3 months status post shunt surgery.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who returns in follow up of adult hydrocephalus. This is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery 08/01/2020. He is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa, together they give his history.\n\nI have followed Mr. Watson since May 2020. He presented with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with the spinal catheter protocol in June 2020 and he underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventriculoperitoneal configuration programmed at level 2.0 was placed.\n\nMr. Watson has had no hospitalizations or other illnesses since I last saw him. With respect to his walking, his daughter states that he is now able to walk to the dining room just fine but could not before his surgery. His balance has improved though he still has some walking impairment. With respect to his bladder control, initially there was some improvement, but he has leveled off and he wears a diaper. With respect to his cognition, both Theresa and the patient say that his thinking has improved. The other daughter, Hannah summarized it best according to the two of them, she said “I feel like I can have a normal conversation with him again.” Mr. Watson has had no headaches and no pain at the shunt site or in the abdomen.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nUrinary incontinence.\nAdult hydrocephalus.\nGait impairment.\nCognitive impairment.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional Symptoms: Significant for impaired ability to carry out daily functions. Negative for fever or unintentional weight loss.\n• Genitourinary: Significant for urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Negative for genital sores, decreased or painful output.\n• Neurological: Significant for continued walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Negative for headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.\n• Psychiatric: Negative for depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure 124/80, heart rate is 64, respiratory rate is 18, weight 174 pounds, and pain is 0/10.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional\nMr. Watson is a pleasant and cooperative man who is able to converse easily though his daughter adds some details.\n\nHead and Face\nThe shunt site was clean, dry, and intact and confirmed at a setting of 2.0.\n\nNeurological\nMental status: Tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, concentration, and fund of knowledge. He scored 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. Of note, he was able to get two of the three memory words with cuing and the third one with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling and at that time he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing and only one with multiple choice.\nGait: Tested using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance of score of 12/16 for a total score of 18-20/28. This has slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28.\nCranial Nerves: Pupils are equal. Extraocular movements are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria.\nMotor: Normal for bulk and strength.\nCoordination: Slow for finger-to-nose.\n\nRESULTS\n\nCT scan of the head from 10/15/2020: It shows a frontal horn span at the level of foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with the span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. This was not noticed by the radiologist who stated no extra axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Adult hydrocephalus.\n• Gait impairment.\n• Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n• Cognitive impairment.\n\nMr. Watson has made some improvement with his hydrocephalus since shunt surgery. There is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region. I do not believe that this tiny amount of fluid is symptomatic, and it was not documented by the radiologist when he read the CT scan.\n\nMr. Watson asked me about whether he will be able to drive again. Unfortunately, I think it is unlikely that his speed of movement will improve to a level that he will be able to pass a driver\'s safety evaluation, however, occasionally patients surprise me by improving enough over 9 to 12 months that they are able to pass such a test. I would certainly be happy to recommend such a test if I believe he is likely to pass it and he is always welcome to enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation, however, I think it is exceeding unlikely that he has the capability of passing this rigorous test at this time. I also think it is quite likely he will not regain sufficient speed of motion to pass such a test.\n\nPLAN\n\nI had a long discussion with the patient and his daughter. We are all pleased that he has started to make some improvement with his hydrocephalus because of the fluid collection in the right parietal region, I would like to leave the setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing the shunt.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow up in 3 months.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] I\'m doing okay, but I\'m still having some problems with my memory.\n\n[doctor] I\'m glad to see you\'re here. I know it\'s been a while since your last visit. I\'m Dr. Thompson, and I\'ll be your doctor today. How are you doing?\n\n[patient] I\'m doing alright, but I\'m still having some problems with my memory.\n\n[doctor] I see. Well, let\'s talk a bit more about your symptoms. How have you been doing since your shunt surgery in August?\n\n[patient] It\'s been a little better. I\'m still having some memory problems, but I\'m able to walk a little better.\n\n[doctor] That\'s great to hear. Can you tell me more about that?\n\n[patient] Yeah, I\'m able to walk to the dining room now, which I couldn\'t do before.\n\n[doctor] That\'s a big improvement. How about your balance? Has that improved as well?\n\n[patient] Yes, it\'s a bit better, but I still have some trouble.\n\n[doctor] Okay. And how about your bladder control?\n\n[patient] It was a little better at first, but now I\'ve leveled off and I still have to wear a diaper.\n\n[doctor] Alright. How about your headaches? Do you have any headaches or pain at the shunt site or in your abdomen?\n\n[patient] No, no headaches or pain at all.\n\n[doctor] That\'s good to know. Let\'s talk a bit about your past medical history. You had urinary incontinence, adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, and cognitive impairment. Have you had any other illnesses or hospitalizations since your last visit?\n\n[patient] No, none.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s go through your vital signs. Your blood pressure is 124/80, your heart rate is 64, your respiratory rate is 18, your weight is 174 pounds, and your pain is 0 out of 10. How are you feeling overall?\n\n[patient] I\'m feeling okay, but I\'m still having some problems.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s do a quick physical exam. I\'ll start with your head and face. I see that the shunt site is clean, dry, and intact, and it\'s currently set at 2.0. How are you feeling about your shunt?\n\n[patient] It\'s been okay. I haven\'t had any problems.\n\n[doctor] Great. Now, let\'s check your neurological status. I\'ll test your mental status by asking you some questions. Can you spell the word "world" for me?\n\n[patient] W-O-R-L-D.\n\n[doctor] Excellent. Now, can you give me the dates of the month?\n\n[patient] January 15th.\n\n[doctor] Good. Now, I\'ll ask you some memory questions. Can you remember the names of the days of the week?\n\n[patient] Yes, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday.\n\n[doctor] That\'s correct. Now, I\'ll give you three words and ask you to repeat them back to me. The words are "dog," "cat," and "bird." Can you remember these words?\n\n[patient] Dog, cat, bird.\n\n[doctor] Great. Now, I\'ll give you a third option. You can choose one of the words that I didn\'t mention. Can you remember one of those words?\n\n[patient] Yes, dog.\n\n[doctor] Excellent. Now, let\'s check your gait. We\'ll use the Tinetti assessment tool. Can you walk for me without an assistive device?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can walk.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s start. Take a few steps.\n\n[patient] (walks)\n\n[doctor] How are you feeling?\n\n[patient] A bit unsteady.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Now, I\'ll check your balance. Can you stand on one foot?\n\n[patient] (stands on one foot)\n\n[doctor] Good. Now, I\'ll check your coordination. Can you touch your nose with your finger?\n\n[patient] (tries to touch nose)\n\n[doctor] Not quite. It\'s a bit slow. Now, let\'s check your cranial nerves. Can you follow my finger with your eyes?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can do that.\n\n[doctor] Excellent. Now, let\'s check your motor function. Can you move your arms and legs?\n\n[patient] Yes, I can move them.\n\n[doctor] Good. Now, let\'s look at the results of your CT scan from October 15th. We can see a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Munro of 4.6 cm with a 3rd ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. There is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with just a tiny subdural collection. The radiologist didn\'t notice this tiny amount of fluid, but I think it\'s not causing any symptoms.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] Based on your current symptoms and the test results, it seems like you\'ve made some improvement with your hydrocephalus since the surgery. There is a tiny fluid collection in the right parietal region, but I don\'t think it\'s causing any symptoms.\n\n[patient] Alright.\n\n[doctor] I think it\'s unlikely that you\'ll be able to drive again. Occasionally, patients surprise me by improving enough to pass a driver\'s safety evaluation, but I think it\'s unlikely in your case. However, you can always enroll in a driver\'s safety program without my recommendation.\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] I\'ll leave the shunt setting at 2.0 for another three months before we consider changing it. Let\'s schedule a follow-up appointment in three months.\n\n[patient] Alright, that sounds good.\n\n[doctor] Do you have any other questions?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s it.\n\n[doctor] Great. I\'ll see you in three months.\n\n[patient] Thank you, doctor.\n\n[doctor] Take care, and see you soon.\n\n[patient] Thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] so christopher watson is an eighty six year old male who returns in follow up of adult hydrocephalus this is his first visit since undergoing shunt surgery on august first twenty twenty he is accompanied by his daughter theresa together they give his history\n[patient] my name is christopher watson i\'m eighty six years old\n[doctor] and your daughter is theresa\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] so i\'ve followed you since may twenty twenty you presented with eight to ten years of progressive gait impairment cognitive impairment and decreased bladder control we established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with the spinal catheter protocol in june twenty twenty and you underwent shunt surgery on august first twenty twenty a medtronic strata programmable shunt in the ventriculoperitoneal configuration programmed at level two point zero was placed\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so you\'ve had no hospitalizations or other illnesses since i last saw you with respect to your walking your daughter says that you are now able to walk to the dining room just fine but could not before your surgery your balance has improved though you still have some walking impairment with respect to your bladder control initially there was some improvement but you have leveled off and you wear a diaper with respect to your cognition both theresa and the patient say that your thinking has improved the other daughter hannah summarized it best according to the two of them she said i feel like i can have a normal conversation with him again you\'ve had no headaches and no pain at the shunt site or in the abdomen\n[patient] no\n[doctor] so let\'s talk a little bit about your past history do you have a history of urinary incontinence\n[patient] yes i do\n[doctor] okay and you had adult hydrocephalus and gait impairment and cognitive impairment\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay so let\'s talk a little bit about your review of systems are you having any fever\n[patie