ot attended physical therapy.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal\nLower right extremity: Swollen right knee, slightly reduced range of motion. Skin warm to touch. Lower right leg pits with pressure.\n\nPROCEDURE\n\nFluid aspiration and Steroid injection.\nRisks, benefits, and alternatives were explained. The risks of steroid injection were explained, including but not limited to, hypopigmentation, soft tissue atrophy, tendon ruptures, and transient hyperglycemia among others. Verbal consent was obtained. The right knee was accessed through the lateral approach with 3 mL of initially clear fluid then blood-tinged fluid. Then the right knee was prepped in a standard fashion. The right knee was injected with 80 mg triamcinolone and 1 mL lidocaine. The procedure was tolerated well with no complications. Post procedure instructions were given.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis management.\n• High risk medication use.\n• Right knee pain.\n• Lower extremity pitting edema.\n\nPLAN\n\nRheumatoid arthritis management.\nThe patient will continue Xeljanz XR 11 mg daily. He will also continue Celebrex 200 mg daily.\n\nHigh-risk medication use.\nThe patient is currently on the high-risk medication Xeljanz. We will check his CMP levels and monitor periodically every couple of months.\n\nRight knee pain.\nHe has right knee pain and swelling, with pain level 6/10. On exam today, the right knee continues to be hot, which is ongoing inflammation. We performed a fluid aspiration of the right knee today, see procedure note above. A steroid injection to the right knee was given today, see procedure note above. We will arrange for x-ray of the right knee and refer his to physical therapy.\n\nLower extremity pitting edema.\nThe patient notes whole right leg swelling since the last visit. He usually wears support hose, which helps to some degree. He has not seen his PCP. We discussed compression stockings, elevate feet, and a low salt diet. He will check with his PCP for further instructions.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, nelson how are you doing today?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay, but i\'ve been having some issues with my knee.\n[doctor] i\'m sorry to hear that. what\'s been going on with your knee?\n[patient] i\'ve been having a lot of pain and swelling in my right knee.\n[doctor] okay, how would you rate the pain?\n[patient] it\'s about a 6 out of 10.\n[doctor] okay. and you said it\'s been going on for a while now?\n[patient] yeah, since the last time you saw me.\n[doctor] okay. and does it get worse at all?\n[patient] yeah, when i stand up after sitting for a while, i have to use a cane to get my bearings.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been able to do any activities?\n[patient] not really. i\'ve been taking it easy.\n[doctor] okay. have you tried anything to help with the pain?\n[patient] no, i\'ve been taking some ibuprofen but it doesn\'t really help.\n[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms like redness or warmth?\n[patient] yeah, my whole right leg is swollen.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been wearing support hose?\n[patient] yeah, i wear support hose but it helps to some degree.\n[doctor] okay. and have you tried any physical therapy?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. have you seen your primary care physician?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t.\n[doctor] okay. well, let\'s take a look at your knee. so your knee is swollen and warm to touch. you do have a slightly reduced range of motion. i can feel some swelling when i press on your lower right leg.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] have you noticed any other areas of swelling?\n[patient] no, just my right leg.\n[doctor] okay. well, we\'ll go ahead and do a fluid aspiration of your knee. we\'ll inject some steroids into your knee and see if that helps. we\'ll also get an x-ray of your knee.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] and i\'m going to refer you to physical therapy.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] and i\'ll continue you on your current medication