-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m okay. i\'ve been having some stomach pain.\n[doctor] okay, so you\'re having some stomach pain. can you tell me more about that?\n[patient] yeah, it\'s been going on for about a week now. it\'s in the right side of my abdomen.\n[doctor] okay. is it a sharp pain or a dull pain?\n[patient] it\'s a dull pain.\n[doctor] okay. and is it constant or does it come and go?\n[patient] it\'s constant.\n[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any nausea with it?\n[patient] yes, i\'ve been feeling nauseous.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been vomiting?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t been vomiting.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any fevers?\n[patient] i\'ve had low-grade fevers.\n[doctor] okay. and any chills?\n[patient] no chills.\n[doctor] okay. have you noticed any changes in your bowel movements or any blood in your stool?\n[patient] no, nothing like that.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any changes in your appetite?\n[patient] no, my appetite has been fine.\n[doctor] okay. and any weight loss?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t lost any weight.\n[doctor] okay. all right. and have you had any recent travel or contact with anyone who\'s been sick?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t traveled recently and nobody\'s been sick around me.\n[doctor] okay. all right. have you had any history of gallstones?\n[patient] yes, i have.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any other symptoms?\n[patient] just the pain and nausea.\n[doctor] okay. any other symptoms like vomiting or chills?\n[patient] no, just the pain and nausea.\n[doctor] okay. have you had any abdominal pain in the past?\n[patient] yes, i had a gallbladder attack a few years ago and they did a lap cholecystectomy.\n[doctor] okay. all right. have you had any other surgeries?\n[patient] yes, i had a surgery for my gallbladder.\n[doctor] okay. and how long ago was that?\n[patient] a few years ago.\n[doctor] okay. all right. and how are you managing your diabetes?\n[patient] i\'m managing it with diet and exercise.\n[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any changes in your diabetes recently?\n[patient] no, my diabetes has been pretty stable.\n[doctor] okay. all right. let\'s go ahead and do a physical exam. your vital signs look good. your blood pressure is 128 over 88, your respiratory rate is 16, your heart rate is 68, your oxygen saturation is 99 percent on room air, and your body temperature is afebrile. let\'s go ahead and do a quick exam of your abdomen. your abdomen is soft and non-distended. there is some slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but there is no rebound tenderness. there is a positive murphy\'s sign, but no peritoneal signs. i do appreciate some mild thickening of the gallbladder. there are some bowel sounds present in all four quadrants. i\'m gon na go ahead and review the ultrasound that you had done. it shows multiple gallstones in your gallbladder and some mild thickening of the gallbladder. the common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 millimeters. it looks like you have some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. i do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like your gallbladder is inflamed. this has probably happened over the last few weeks or since you\'ve been symptomatic. i do believe you should have a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. i\'ll describe the surgery to you. the surgery will take about an hour to an hour and a half, and you\'ll probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. you should avoid high-fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. all your questions were answered. do you have any questions about the surgery?\n[patient] no, i think i understand.\n[doctor] okay. i\'ll also recommend that you refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period, but you may return to walking outdoors on solid ground a week status post-surgery. i also want you to follow up with your doctor regarding your impending surgery. we\'ll continue to monitor your diabetes as has been outlined by either your endocrinologist or your primary care provider. do you have any questions about managing your diabetes?\n[patient] no, i think i\'m managing it okay.\n[doctor] okay. all right. i\'ll see you in a week status post-surgery. do you have any other questions?\n[patient] no, i think that\'s it.\n[doctor] okay. great. take care.\n[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] good morning\n[patient] good morning\n[doctor] good morning i\'m dr smith and i\'m here with mr johnson who is a 52 year old male who presents today with abdominal pain\n[patient] hi\n[doctor] uh so mr johnson here for abdominal pain\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] and so how are you doing today\n[patient] i\'m doing okay\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] i\'m having some pain\n[doctor] yeah okay and so what type of pain are you having\n[patient] it\'s in my right side\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] of my stomach\n[doctor] okay and how long has this been going on for\n[patient] about three days\n[doctor] okay and what kind of symptoms are you having with this\n[patient] just like nausea and vomiting\n[doctor] okay and nausea and vomiting yeah\n[patient] and then i have some pain in my right side of my stomach\n[doctor] okay and you said nausea and vomiting do you have a fever\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and when did you start having these symptoms\n[patient] a few days ago\n[doctor] okay and have you had this before\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and so what have you done for it so far\n[patient] i\'ve been drinking water and eating a lot of crackers\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] and i\'ve been trying to stay hydrated\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] i\'ve been trying to stay hydrated\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] and i\'ve been trying to stay hydrated\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] and i\'ve been trying to stay hydrated\n[doctor] okay and so how are you doing with your diabetes\n[patient] i\'m doing good\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] i\'m doing good\n[doctor] okay and have you been taking your medications\n[patient] yes i have\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] yeah i have\n[doctor] okay and how are you managing your diabetes\n[patient] i\'m doing good\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] i\'m doing good\n[doctor] okay and so have you been eating well\n[patient] i\'ve been eating well\n[doctor] okay and how about exercising\n[patient] i\'ve been exercising\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] yeah i\'ve been exercising\n[doctor] okay and so you enjoy hiking\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other medical problems\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and so any family history of medical problems\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay and what is that\n[patient] my mom and dad both had gallstones\n[doctor] okay and did they have surgery for it\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay and so what kind of surgery did they have\n[patient] they had cholecystectomies\n[doctor] okay and so what do you think caused your symptoms\n[patient] i\'m not sure\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] i\'m not sure\n[doctor] okay and so i\'m gon na go ahead and do a physical exam and some vitals\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay so on general exam you appear well-appearing today in no distress\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] on cardiovascular exam your heart is regular rate and rhythm no ectopic beats no murmurs clicks or rubs\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] on respiratory exam your lungs are clear bilaterally\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] on abdominal exam you have a soft nondistended abdomen with some slight guarding to the right upper quadrant but without rebound tenderness and you do have a positive murphy\'s sign but no peritoneal signs appreciated\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] on abdominal ultrasound you have multiple gallstones in your gallbladder and you do have some mild thickening of the gallbladder and your common bile duct size is within normal limits at four point one millimeters\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so based on my exam and your vitals and your history i think you do have some mild cholecystitis with gallstones and i do not appreciate any frank obstruction but it looks like your gallbladder is inflamed and this has probably been going on for a few weeks or since you\'ve been symptomatic\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so i think the best course of treatment for you would be a laparoscopic cholecystectomy\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and so we will have to do that surgery and i\'ll have my nurse come in and we can answer any questions you have and we can schedule that surgery for you\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and so do you have any questions for me\n[patient] yes i do\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] so i was wondering what kind of surgery is this\n[doctor] it\'s a laparoscopic cholecystectomy so it\'s a minimally invasive surgery\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] where we\'ll make a few small incisions in your belly and put a camera and some instruments inside to remove your gallbladder\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and you\'ll be asleep during the procedure and you\'ll be in the hospital for a few hours and then you\'ll go home\n[patient] okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and so you\'ll be able to eat whatever you want but i would recommend avoiding high fat foods and eating foods that are high in fiber\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and then we\'ll have you follow up with me one week status post surgery\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and i would recommend not hiking for the first week and then you can start walking on solid ground after that\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and so do you have any other questions for me\n[patient] no\n[docto