rt?\n[patient] yes, that hurts.\n[doctor] okay. and can you move your arm for me?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so you can move it up and down?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] and then out to the side?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and then back and forth?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and what about this way?\n[patient] that hurts.\n[doctor] okay. and what about this way?\n[patient] that hurts too.\n[doctor] okay. let\'s check your neck. does that hurt?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t hurt.\n[doctor] okay. let\'s check your heart and lungs. does that hurt?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t hurt.\n[doctor] okay. and let\'s check your head. does that hurt?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t hurt.\n[doctor] okay. so i\'m gon na check your vital signs. your blood pressure is 122 over 76, your heart rate is 82, and your oxygen saturation is 95 on room air. so let\'s take a look at your x-rays. so your x-ray shows an acute fracture of the acromioclavicular joint. so what does that mean?\n[patient] i\'m not sure.\n[doctor] okay. so your acromioclavicular joint is the joint where the collarbone meets the shoulder blade. so what\'s happening is that the ligaments that hold that joint together have been stretched or torn, which is causing your pain and limited movement. so what i\'m gon na do is put you in a sling and prescribe meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day to help with the pain. i also want to get a ct scan of your shoulder to get a better look at the fracture. and then i want you to start formal physical therapy to strengthen your shoulder, which will hopefully help you avoid surgery in the future. and i think it\'s a good idea to avoid playing tackle football for now. does that sound good to you?\n[patient] yes, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. great. i\'ll get my nurse to come in and help you get into the sling. do you have any questions?\n[patient] no, that\'s all.\n[doctor] okay. all right, well, i\'ll see you in a couple of days to check on you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n