-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] good morning how are you\n[patient] good morning doctor\n[doctor] so scott how are you doing\n[patient] i\'m doing okay\n[doctor] so you are here for a right knee injury\n[patient] yeah i was playing soccer over the weekend and i felt a pop in my knee\n[doctor] okay so you were playing soccer and you felt a pop in your knee\n[patient] yeah and it\'s been swelling up\n[doctor] okay so you\'ve had some swelling\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] and how long ago was this\n[patient] about three days ago\n[doctor] okay so you said you felt a pop in your knee and you\'ve had some swelling since then\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] and how would you rate the pain level\n[patient] probably a six or seven out of ten\n[doctor] okay and is that a six or seven all the time or does it vary\n[patient] it varies it\'s a four or five when the swelling goes down\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] but when it\'s at its worst it\'s a six or seven\n[doctor] okay and what makes the pain better\n[patient] just taking ibuprofen\n[doctor] okay and what makes the pain worse\n[patient] just moving my knee\n[doctor] okay and how would you describe the movement of your knee\n[patient] it\'s not as functional as it used to be\n[doctor] okay so it\'s not moving as well as it used to\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] and you said you felt a pop in your knee and you\'ve had some swelling since then\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] and are you able to bend your knee\n[patient] i can bend it but it hurts towards the end of it\n[doctor] okay and are you able to straighten it\n[patient] i have to help it straighten\n[doctor] okay and are you able to bear weight on it\n[patient] i can but it feels loose and it doesn\'t feel normal\n[doctor] okay so you\'re able to bear weight but it doesn\'t feel right\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so let\'s talk a little bit about your past medical history\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so do you have any other injuries or surgeries in the past\n[patient] no i\'ve had some problems with my back but nothing with my knee\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other health problems\n[patient] i have high blood pressure\n[doctor] okay so you have high blood pressure\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and do you take any medications for that\n[patient] yeah i do\n[doctor] okay and are you on any medications for the knee\n[patient] i\'ve been taking ibuprofen\n[doctor] okay and how often have you been taking it\n[patient] pretty much daily\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other surgeries\n[patient] no i just had my wisdom teeth taken out\n[doctor] okay and how about any other injuries\n[patient] just my back\n[doctor] okay so you\'ve had some problems with your back\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so let\'s talk a little bit about your social history\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so do you work\n[patient] i work at a desk\n[doctor] okay and how long have you been working at a desk\n[patient] about five years\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other activities that you do\n[patient] i like to play soccer\n[doctor] okay and you were playing soccer when you got hurt\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other hobbies\n[patient] i like to play basketball\n[doctor] okay and have you been able to play basketball\n[patient] no i haven\'t been able to play since i hurt my knee\n[doctor] okay so let\'s talk a little bit about your review of systems\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so any muscle or joint pain anywhere else\n[patient] just my right knee\n[doctor] okay and any other symptoms\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay so i\'m going to go ahead and examine you now\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so i\'m going to start by just looking at your knee\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so i see that there is some ecchymosis and some swelling\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and i\'m going to press on your knee here\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so i\'m feeling some pain at the medial joint line\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and i\'m going to move your knee around\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so i\'m feeling some pain with that\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so i\'m going to test your knee ligaments\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so i\'m going to push on your knee here\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] and i\'m going to push it forward\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and i\'m going to push it back\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so i\'m feeling some laxity\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so let\'s talk a little bit about my assessment and plan\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so i think that you may have a possible anterior cruciate ligament tear\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and i\'m going to recommend that we get an mri of your knee\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and i\'m going to prescribe you some meloxicam 15 milligrams a day\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and i want you to use ice and activity modification to reduce the swelling and pain\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and i want you to wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when you\'re ambulating\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and i want you to avoid jogging until we get the results of the mri\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and we\'ll follow up with you once we have the results of the mri\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] so does that sound good\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay great\n[patient] thank you }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] scott miller is a pleasant thirty six year old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. the injury occurred three days ago when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. he reports swelling at the time of the injury which has improved. the patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. he rates his pain level as a six to seven out of ten currently and a four to five out of ten when the swelling decreases. the patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis which provides some relief. he states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion however when he goes to straighten his knee he feels like he has to help it straight. the patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee however it feels loose and does not feel normal. }} {{ [doctor] scott miller is a pleasant thirty six year old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. the injury occurred three days ago when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. he