[92mlitellm.acompletion(model='gpt-4o', api_base=None, messages=[{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Price is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 2.5 weeks ago, when he stepped off of a curb and turned over his ankle. He locates his pain to the lateral aspect of his right knee. The patient also reports clicking with ambulation. He denies any pain with ambulation unless he stands on one foot for an extended period of time. The patient also reports locking of his knee with prolonged standing. He notices pain when he is resting his foot on his heel. He denies any pain at rest and elevated, or when his foot is flat on the ground. The patient states that he is more active during the day, which does not impact his knee pain. He adds that he is able to perform a full squat without pain. The patient reports numbness and tingling in his right foot, however this is not new. The patient has been taking ibuprofen, which provides him with relief at night. He has also been icing his knee 4 times per day, which provides him with relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nHe states that he has fractured his 2nd toe 3 times over the last 10 years.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works in sales. He rides a bike for exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nNeurological: Positive for numbness and tingling in his toes on his right foot from prior injury.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: >Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight edema. Slight effusion. No pain with palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full ROM. Negative Lachman\'s.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the right knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormality. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee lateral collateral ligament strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays did not reveal any acute fractures or bony abnormality. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen his right knee. I have also prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, gregory. how are you doing today?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay. i\'m here for my right knee. i\'ve been having a lot of pain.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s start with your knee. so, what happened to your knee?\n[patient] i stepped off of a curb and turned over my ankle.\n[doctor] okay. so, i think you\'re saying that you hurt your ankle.\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and then you turned over your ankle, which means you hurt the outside of your knee, correct?\n[patient] yeah, that\'s right.\n[doctor] okay. and how long ago did this happen?\n[patient] it was about two and a half weeks ago.\n[doctor] okay. and how would you describe the pain? is it a sharp pain, a dull pain, or a burning pain?\n[patient] it\'s a dull pain.\n[doctor] okay. and where exactly is the pain located?\n[patient] on the outside of my knee.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you walk?\n[patient] it doesn\'t hurt when i walk, but when i stand on one foot for a long time, it hurts.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you stand flat on the ground?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you\'re sitting?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t.\n[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you\'re lying down?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you bend your knee?\n[patient] no, i can bend it without any pain.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you straighten your knee?\n[patient] no, that\'s okay too.\n[doctor] okay. okay. so, what does hurt?\n[patient] it hurts when i stand on my heel.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you rest your foot on your heel?\n[patient] yes, it does.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you rest your foot on your heel and your knee is bent?\n[patient] no, that\'s okay.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does it hurt when you rest your foot on your heel and your knee is straight?\n[patient] yes, that\'s when it hurts the most.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms, such as clicking or locking of your knee?\n[patient] yeah, i do notice some clicking when i walk.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and does your knee lock up when you\'re standing?\n[patient] yes, it does lock up when i\'m standing for a long time.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and have you noticed any numbness or tingling in your foot?\n[patient] yeah, i do notice some numbness and tingling in my foot.\n[doctor] okay. okay. is that a new symptom?\n[patient] no, it\'s been there for a while.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and what have you been doing for the pain?\n[patient] i\'ve been taking ibuprofen, which gives me relief at night. i\'ve also been icing it four times a day, which gives me relief in the morning and at the end of the day.\n[doctor] okay. okay. okay. have you had any other injuries to your knee in the past?\n[patient] i\'ve fractured my second toe three times over the last ten years.\n[doctor] okay. okay. and do you have a job?\n[patient] i work in sales.\n[doctor] okay. and do you do any exercise?\n[patient] i ride a bike.\n[doctor] okay. okay. let me just go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i\'m going to start with your cardiovascular exam. your pulse is strong and equal in both legs. i\'m going to check your neurological exam. i do appreciate some numbness and tingling in your right foot, which is consistent with your past injury. let\'s go ahead and move on to your musculoskeletal exam. i\'m going to check your right knee. i do appreciate some swelling and some fluid in the knee. i\'m going to check the ligaments. your varus test is positive, which means you have some ligamentous laxity. i\'m going to check your range of motion. your knee is nice and stable. you can bend it without any pain. you can straighten it without any pain. i\'m going to check your lachman\'s test, which is a test for an acl tear, and that is negative. so, let\'s talk about the x-rays. i reviewed the x-rays today. they show no evidence of any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormalities. so, based on my examination and your x-rays, it sounds like you have a lateral collateral ligament strain. i\'m going to recommend that we treat you conservatively with formal physical therapy to strengthen your right knee. i\'m also going to prescribe you meloxicam 15 milligrams to help with your pain. if your pain does not improve with physical therapy, we\'ll consider getting an mri. does that sound okay with you?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any questions?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any other questions?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. i\'ll go ahead and get my nurse to come in and we\'ll get you checked out.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. thank you.\n[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, gregory. how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing well. how are you?\n[doctor] good. so gregory, you\'re here today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. so tell me a little bit about what happened.\n[patient] well, i stepped off of a curb and i turned over my ankle and i fell down.\n[doctor] okay. and where do you feel the pain?\n[patient] it\'s on the outside of my knee.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any pain with ambulation?\n[patient] i do, but it\'s not all the time. only when i\'m standing on one foot for a long time.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any pain when you\'re standing?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and any pain when you\'re elevated?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and when you\'re flat on the ground?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and any pain with locking of the knee?\n[patient] yes, when i stand for a long time.\n[doctor] okay. and do you feel any pain when you\'re resting your foot on your heel?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and do you feel any pain at rest?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and any pain when you\'re on one foot for a long time?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and do you feel any numbness and tingling in your right foot?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and is this new?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any similar injuries in the past?\n[patient] no, not like this.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any medical problems?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any surger