eports increased drainage and fatigue. She states that she has been wearing her regular shoes.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes with neuropathy. She states that her blood sugars are running better than they were; however, they are still in the 200\'s. Her last hemoglobin A1C was 10.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nSkin: Reports right foot ulcer and drainage.\n\nVITALS\n\nNo fever noted. Vitals look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right foot: There is a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. I do see some yellow slough that is present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection. I do not see necrosis. No odor. I do not appreciate any bony exposure. No pain to palpation.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right foot today reveals no evidence of osteomyelitis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight diabetic foot ulcer.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have recommended that we obtain an ankle brachial index to determine her blood supply to see if we can heal the wound. We discussed doing a debridement in office today, which she agreed to. I have also recommended that the patient be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. I have also prescribed the patient collagenase ointment to apply to her wound 1 time per day and cover it with a sterile dressing. I advised the patient that this should be continued until we see the ulcer decrease in size.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to check on her progress.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi christina how are you\n[patient] i\'m okay\n[doctor] good to see you christina so i\'m looking at your chart and it looks like you\'re here for a right foot ulcer that\'s been present for a year now how are you doing with that\n[patient] it\'s still here\n[doctor] okay so it started a year ago and it\'s just been persistent so i\'m glad you\'re here so what happened with the onset of it\n[patient] i don\'t know it just happened it was a blister\n[doctor] okay and then it just turned red and then what did you do\n[patient] i picked at it\n[doctor] okay and then what happened after that\n[patient] and it just got red\n[doctor] okay and then what did you do after that\n[patient] i went to my doctor and she gave me some antibiotics\n[doctor] okay and what was the antibiotic\n[patient] keflex\n[doctor] okay and did that help\n[patient] it did a little bit\n[doctor] a little bit okay and then what happened after that\n[patient] it did get a little better but it\'s still there\n[doctor] okay and then you said that you were on keflex five months ago so it\'s been here for a year\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay and have you had any pain with it\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and any other symptoms\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and how about your sensation in your foot\n[patient] it\'s decreased\n[doctor] okay and any drainage\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] and any fatigue\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay and how about your diet and exercise have you been watching your diet\n[patient] i\'ve been trying to watch my diet\n[doctor] okay and any exercise\n[patient] i\'ve been walking\n[doctor] okay and how about your diabetes how are you doing with that\n[patient] my blood sugars are running better than they were but they\'re still in the 200s\n[doctor] okay and what was your last hemoglobin a1c\n[patient] 10\n[doctor] okay so i\'m gon na go ahead and examine your foot so i\'m gon na start with your right foot so i\'m gon na look at your right foot and i do see a one centimeter by two centimeter wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot just proximal to the fifth mtp joint and i do see some yellow slough that\'s present with minimal granulation tissue no surrounding erythema or cellulitis no evidence of fluid collection no necrosis no odor no bony exposure no pain to palpation i do appreciate some 