ight radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] good morning, how are you?\n[patient] good morning, i\'m doing alright.\n[doctor] hey, so i know you came in for a follow-up, how are you feeling?\n[patient] i\'m doing alright, i\'m just trying to get back into my normal routine.\n[doctor] okay, so i reviewed your records, and i see that you had a heart attack a while back. how have you been doing since then?\n[patient] i\'ve been doing well. i\'ve been taking all my medications, so that\'s good.\n[doctor] that\'s great. and you\'ve been doing okay with your heart?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been doing alright. i haven\'t had any chest pain or anything like that.\n[doctor] okay, that\'s good to hear. and how about shortness of breath?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t had any shortness of breath.\n[doctor] okay, and any leg swelling?\n[patient] yeah, i do have some leg swelling, but i think that\'s just from sitting around a lot.\n[doctor] okay, let\'s go ahead and take a look at your vitals, and then we\'ll do a quick physical exam. so your vitals look good. your heart rate and blood pressure are within normal limits.\n[doctor] okay, let\'s go ahead and listen to your heart. so, i do hear a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base, but that\'s not uncommon after a heart attack. i don\'t hear any carotid bruits. your radial artery cath site looks good. it\'s clean, dry, and intact without any hematoma. your radial artery pulses are palpable. let\'s go ahead and check your lungs. so, your lungs are clear bilaterally. and on your neck exam, there is no jugular venous distention. let\'s go ahead and check your lower extremities. so, you do have some bilateral lower limb edema. i don\'t see any pitting edema, but you do have some swelling in your legs. i\'ll go ahead and review your ekg. so, your ekg looks good. it shows a normal sinus rhythm with good r wave progression. and i\'ll go ahead and review your echocardiogram. so, your echocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35 %. let\'s talk about that a little bit. so, you had a heart attack, and it looks like your heart muscle was injured, but it\'s not as bad as we thought it would be. so, i think it will improve over time. and you were able to get treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so that\'s good. let\'s talk about your medications. you\'ve been doing well with taking your aspirin, brilinta, lipitor, toprol, and lisinopril. i\'m going to continue you on those medications. i\'m going to refer you to cardiac rehab to help you get back to exercising regularly.\n[doctor] so, let\'s talk about your new heart failure. so, you\'ve been retaining fluid, and that\'s why you\'ve had some leg swelling. i\'m going to prescribe lasix 40 mg once a day. i\'m also going to add aldactone 12.5 mg daily. i\'ll order some lab work and we\'ll get a repeat echo in about two months.\n[doctor] let\'s talk about your hypertension. your blood pressure is well controlled. you\'ll tolerate the aldactone well. i\'ll encourage you to continue exercising and be mindful of your diet. there are healthier alternatives to reduce the amount of preserved meats you consume. you received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider other diet options. i\'ll continue you on lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n[doctor] let\'s talk about your diabetes. it\'s stable. i\'ll encourage you to continue monitoring your blood glucose levels. you\'ll continue metformin as prescribed. do you have any questions or concerns about the plan?\n[patient] no, i think you covered everything.\n[doctor] okay, great. you\'ll follow up as instructed. take care.\n[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] patient is here for a follow up of her status post inferior stemi\n[doctor] i know you had your stent put in last year and i know you\'re doing well\n[patient] yes i am\n[doctor] okay so i know you\'re doing well but i do want to do a quick physical exam and a quick review of your vitals\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay so let\'s start with your vitals are they the same as they were last time you were in here\n[patient] yeah they\'re pretty similar\n[doctor] okay and you\'re not having any chest pain\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and shortness of breath\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and any leg swelling\n[patient] yeah\n[doctor] okay so let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay so on your neck exam there\'s no jugular venous distention\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay and on your respiratory exam your lungs are clear bilaterally\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and on your cardiovascular exam there\'s no carotid bruits and a three out of six systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left base\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] and on your extremity exam your right radial artery cath site is clean dry and intact without hematoma and your radial artery pulses are palpable\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay so i did review your echo and i know you had a stent put in and i know you\'re doing well\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] i know that you had a stent put in last year and i know you\'re doing well\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] and i do want to do a quick review of your medications are you still taking the same medications\n[patient] yes i am\n[doctor] okay so i know that you\'re still taking the aspirin 81 milligrams a day and the brilinta 90 milligrams twice a day and the lipitor 80 milligrams a day and the toprol 50 milligrams a day and the lisino