ks on a keyboard all day.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she has been taking ibuprofen.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies sleep disturbance.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports pain in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\nNeurological: Reports numbness and tingling in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd digits on the right hand.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right hand: Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist.. Decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. Positive Tinel\'s sign.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight carpal tunnel syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing her symptoms and exam findings, I believe her symptoms are secondary to carpal tunnel syndrome. We discussed the nature of this condition, as well as her treatment options, in detail. I recommend we treat this conservatively with nighttime wrist splinting and ibuprofen, but I want to increase her dose to 600 mg 4 times daily. I\'m also going to order an EMG/Nerve conduction study for further evaluation. If this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, alexis, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] good to see you. so, what brings you in today?\n[patient] i\'ve been having some pain in my right hand.\n[doctor] okay. so, what kind of pain are you having?\n[patient] it\'s mostly in my wrist.\n[doctor] okay. and when did you first start noticing this?\n[patient] about six months ago.\n[doctor] okay. and how long has it been constant?\n[patient] well, it\'s been constant for the past month or so.\n[doctor] okay. and what kind of symptoms are you having? is it numbness or tingling?\n[patient] yeah, it\'s mostly numbness and tingling in my first, second, and third fingers on my right hand.\n[doctor] okay. and how long does this last?\n[patient] it\'s constant.\n[doctor] okay. and how has this been affecting your daily activities?\n[patient] it\'s hard to type on my keyboard and use the mouse.\n[doctor] okay. and does this affect your sleep?\n[patient] no, it doesn\'t.\n[doctor] okay. so, you\'re working on a keyboard all day. have you tried any treatment?\n[patient] i\'ve been taking ibuprofen.\n[doctor] okay. and does that help?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and have you tried anything else?\n[patient] just ibuprofen.\n[doctor] okay. let\'s do a quick physical exam. i\'m gon na start with your right hand. so, i\'m gon na press on the nerves in your wrist here. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na press on your fingers here. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na move your wrist up and down. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na bend it down like this. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na bend it up like this. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na squeeze your hand. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na check your grip strength. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. and i\'m gon na check your sensation here. does that hurt?\n[patient] yeah, it does.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s talk a little bit about what i\'ve found. so, i do appreciate some pain with flexion and extension of your wrist, and i do appreciate decreased grip strength on the right compared to the left. i also appreciate a positive tinel\'s sign. so, what i think is going on is you have carpal tunnel syndrome.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] so, we can treat this conservatively. i\'m gon na recommend that you wear a nighttime wrist splint to help alleviate some of the symptoms. i\'m also gon na increase the dose of ibuprofen to 600 milligrams four times a day.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] and i\'m gon na order an emg/nerve conduction study to further evaluate the nerve damage. if this is positive, we can consider additional treatment with physical therapy or surgical intervention with carpal tunnel release. does that sound good?\n[patient] yeah, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any questions?\n[patient] no, that\'s it.\n[doctor] okay. great. well, i\'m gon na get you scheduled for the emg/nerve conduction study. my nurse will be in shortly to do that. do you have any other questions?\n[patient] no, i think that\'s it.\n[doctor] okay. great. well, i\'m gon na see you out in the waiting room. thanks.\n[patient] thanks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hey there alexis how are you\n[patient] i\'m doing okay\n[doctor] okay good to see you so what brings you in here today\n[patient] i have this weird pain in my right hand\n[doctor] okay and when did this start\n[patient] it started about six months ago\n[doctor] okay and how would you describe the pain\n[patient] um i would say it\'s numbness and tingling in my first second and third fingers of my right hand\n[doctor] okay and is it constant or is it intermittent\n[patient] it\'s constant\n[doctor] okay and is it constant all the time or is it coming and going\n[patient] it\'s coming and going\n[doctor] okay and does it go away with rest\n[patient] yes it does\n[doctor] okay and does it come back with activity\n[patient] yes it does\n[doctor] okay and what kind of activities are you doing when you\'re experiencing this pain\n[patient] i\'m using the computer and typing on the keyboard\n[doctor] okay and do you have to hold the mouse\n[patient] yes\n[doctor] okay and how much time do you spend on the computer\n[patient] all day\n[doctor] okay and are you having any sleep disturbance\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and what have you tried for the pain so far\n[patient] i\'ve tried ibuprofen\n[doctor] okay and has that been helpful\n[patient] yes it has been helpful\n[doctor] okay and how much ibuprofen are you taking\n[patient] i\'ve been taking two 200 milligrams twice a day\n[