a repeat hemoglobin a1c to check for any needed medication adjustments, and i want you to follow up with me in two weeks. for your chest pain, i suspect that it might be unstable angina, considering your history of diabetes, your possible history of hypertension, and your family history of heart disease. your recent ekg was unremarkable, and there are no other signs of heart attack, but i\'m worried that you might have some sort of blockage causing the pain. so, i\'m going to have you start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 milligrams daily, and i\'ve sent in a prescription for that today. i also sent a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed, and i want you to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after about 5 minutes. so, i\'m also going to have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation. and for your hypertension, i know several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in your chart. so, i want you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next two weeks and report your readings to me via mychart. this should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. we discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide, and how this will hopefully reduce some of the swelling that we see on your physical exam. all of your questions were answered, and you understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan. do you have any other questions for me?\n[patient] no, i think that\'s it.\n[doctor] alright, i\'m going to have my nurse come in and get you checked out. she\'ll be with you shortly to get you started on your medications and to get you scheduled for your cardiology follow-up. okay?\n[patient] okay, sounds good. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] good morning, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay. i\'m a little worried about my chest pain.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m doctor smith. i\'m a cardiologist with n