n is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. her pain is alleviated by rest. the patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ace bandage. she also takes ibuprofen or aleve occasionally for pain. she states she tries to be as active as she can. she enjoys running outdoors. the patient adds that she would run five to six miles per day however she is unable to go point five miles secondary to the pain. she denies any previous injuries to her knees. she works from home.\n[doctor] any other medical problems?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] any surgeries?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] do you have any allergies?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] and what about medications?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] all right. so tell me about your knee pain. when did it start?\n[patient] six months ago.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any injuries?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and what does it feel like?\n[patient] it\'s like a deep, achy pain.\n[doctor] okay. and where is it located?\n[patient] it\'s located on the back of my patella.\n[doctor] okay. and is there any swelling or redness?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you move it?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you stand up from a seated position?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you go up and down the stairs?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and is it better when you rest?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and have you tried icing it or anything like that?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and do you wrap it with a bandage?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and do you take any ibuprofen or aleve?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and do you try to stay active?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and what do you like to do?\n[patient] i like to run outside.\n[doctor] okay. and how far can you run?\n[patient] i can run five to six miles a day.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been able to run five to six miles a day lately?\n[patient] no. i can\'t even go point five miles.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any injuries to your knees before?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and do you work from home?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. all right. well, i\'m going to go ahead and do a physical exam. i\'m gon na listen to your heart and lungs. everything sounds good. i\'m gon na do a quick look at your back. everything looks good. i\'m gon na check your lower extremities. your strength is good. i\'m gon na check your reflexes. your reflexes are normal. i\'m gon na check your knees. you do have pain with patella grind. you have pain with squatting. you have no swelling or redness of your knees. and i\'m gon na check for crepitus. you do have crepitus. and i\'m gon na check for any tenderness. you do have tenderness to palpation of your retinaculum. i\'m gon na do a quick look at your x-rays. your x-rays look good. there\'s no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis. all right. so based on my exam and your x-rays, i think you have patellofemoral pain syndrome. and i\'m gon na explain what that is. patellofemoral pain syndrome is when your patella rubs against the femur. it\'s a very common problem. and it can be caused by a number of things. it can be caused by overuse, it can be caused by muscle imbalances, it can be caused by biomechanics. and i\'m gon na explain what i think might be going on with you. i think that you have muscle imbalances. i think that you\'re weak in your lower extremities. i think that you have poor running mechanics. and i think that you\'re overusing your knee. and that\'s why you have this pain. so i\'m gon na recommend that we start with some physical therapy. we can get you into a program to increase your lower extremity strength and mobility. we can work on your running mechanics. and we can also give you some exercises that you can do at home to strengthen your knees. i\'m gon na prescribe you some ibuprofen or aleve. you can take those as needed. and i want you to take it easy for a little while. i want you to take it easy with your running. and then we can reevaluate you in a few weeks. all right?\n[patient] all right. i can do that.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any questions?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. all right. i\'m gon na go ahead and order some physical therapy for you. and then we\'ll see you in a few weeks. all right?\n[patient] all right.\n[doctor] okay. do you have any questions for me?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. all right. i\'ll see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[doctor] hey sophia how are you doing today\n[patient] i\'ve been better my primary care doctor wanted me to see you because of this knee pain that i\'ve been having for about six months now\n[doctor] okay do you remember what caused the pain initially\n[patient] you know i really ca n\'t recall any specific event that caused the pain\n[doctor] alright well can can you describe the pain for me\n[patient] yeah so it\'s it\'s like a deep achy pain that it i feel like it\'s behind my kneecaps and sometimes i can even hear like what seems like like some creaking in my knees\n[doctor] hmmm alright now tell me what what type of activities makes that pain worse\n[patient] so i feel like the pain gets worse anytime i\'m like getting from like a seated position to standing so i i work from home so i\'m at my desk a lot so anytime i get up i have some pain or after watching tv or even like going up and down stairs it\'s really painful\n[doctor] hmmm okay and does anything make the pain feel better\n[patient] usually after i rest it for some time it does help with the pain\n[doctor] okay now you did mention earlier that you have tried some things in the past can you tell me what they were\n[patient] yeah so when it first started hurting i was putting ice on it and i even got like some ace bandage like wrapping from the pharmacy i do take ibuprofen or aleve sometimes but i try to avoid really taking any medications\n[doctor] okay and and te