tape. She states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema and effusion noted. Moderate ROM. Pain with flexion and extension of the knee. Negative Lachman\'s. Negative valgus and varus stress test.\n\nRESULTS\n\n4 views of the left knee were taken. These reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLeft knee pain, possible MCL strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have discussed with the patient that her x-rays did not reveal any signs of a tear. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The plan is to place the patient in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. I have also prescribed the patient tramadol 50 mg to treat her pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have also advised her to ice her left knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If her pain does not improve with the immobilizer, I will recommend obtaining an MRI.\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?\n\n[patient] I\'m doing okay, I\'ve been having some knee pain.\n\n[doctor] Hmm, okay. Can you tell me a little bit more about the knee pain? When did it start?\n\n[patient] It started about a week ago.\n\n[doctor] Okay. What was happening when you first felt the pain?\n\n[patient] I was running around with my niece and I fell. I think I twisted my knee.\n\n[doctor] I see. Did you hear a pop when you fell?\n\n[patient] Yes, I heard a pop.\n\n[doctor] And how would you describe the pain? Is it a throbbing pain?\n\n[patient] Yes, it is.\n\n[doctor] And how would you rate your pain level?\n\n[patient] It\'s about a 4 out of 10.\n\n[doctor] Okay. And how has your pain been since then?\n\n[patient] It\'s been increasing.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Have you been doing anything to treat the pain?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been taking Tylenol and wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.\n\n[doctor] Okay. And have you been able to walk on it?\n\n[patient] I\'ve been limping.\n\n[doctor] Hmm. Have you taken any ibuprofen for the pain?\n\n[patient] No, I can\'t take ibuprofen because I have an ulcer.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Alright. Let me take a look at your knee. Can you lift up your pant leg for me?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] I\'m going to press here. Does that hurt?\n\n[patient] Yes, it does.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let me press here. How about this spot?\n\n[patient] That hurts too.\n\n[doctor] Okay. And how about this one?\n\n[patient] That one hurts the most.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let me take a look at your knee. I\'m going to move your knee around a little bit. Can you flex your knee for me?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] How about extending it?\n\n[patient] It hurts when I do that too.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let\'s do a little bit of a special test called Lachman\'s test. Can you push your knee back and forth like this?\n\n[patient] Sure.\n\n[doctor] And how about if I push it in this direction?\n\n[patient] It doesn\'t hurt as much.\n\n[doctor] Okay. And how about if I push it in this direction?\n\n[patient] It hurts a little bit.\n\n[doctor] Alright. Let\'s do another test called a valgus and varus stress test. Can you bend your knee and let me push your knee in this direction?\n\n[patient] Okay.\n\n[doctor] And how about if I push it in this direction?\n\n[patient] It hurts a little bit.\n\n[doctor] Okay. Let me take a look at the x-ray. Do you have any questions about what we\'ve done so far?\n\n[patient] No, I think you\'ve covered everything.\n\n[doctor] Alright. The x-ray shows no signs of a fracture or dislocation. It looks like you may have a strain of the MCL. We can treat you conservatively. I\'m going to put you in a knee immobilizer to provide support for the next week. I\'m also going to prescribe you some tramadol 50 mg to help with the pain. You can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. We\'ll also have you ice your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. You should refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. If your pain doesn\'t improve with the immobilizer, we may need to get an MRI.\n\n[patient] Okay, that sounds good. Thank you.\n\n[doctor] You\'re welcome. Do you have any other questions?\n\n[patient] No, I think that\'s it.\n\n[doctor] Alright. We\'ll see you in 2 weeks.\n\n[patient] Okay, thank you. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] angela powell is a pleasant 81-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. the onset of her pain began one week ago, when she was running around with her niece and fell. at the time of the injury, she heard a pop and believes that she twisted her knee. she reports that she has been limping since the injury. the patient locates her pain to the medial and lateral aspects of her knee. she rates her pain level as a four out of ten, however this is increasing. her pain is described as a throbbing pain. the patient has been taking tylenol, which has provided mild and temporary relief, as well as wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. she states that she is unable to take ibuprofen secondary to an ulcer.\n[doctor] okay. so, you were running around with your niece and you fell, and you heard a pop. did you hear it pop when you were running or when you were getting up from running?\n[patient] when i was running.\n[doctor] okay. and then you fell. did you fall on your left leg or your right leg?\n[patient] left.\n[doctor] okay. and you heard a pop. did you feel it on the inside or outside of your knee?\n[patient] outside.\n[doctor] okay. and then you\'ve been limping since then. have you been able to walk on it at all?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and you\'ve been limping, and you\'ve been taking tylenol, and wrapping your knee with kinesiology tape. and you\'re not able to take ibuprofen because of an ulcer. is that correct?\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] okay. and you\'ve been having pain on the inside and outside of your knee. is that correct?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. and the pain is throbbing, and it\'s getting worse. is that correct?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] okay. have you ever hurt your knee before?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and are you on any medications?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any surgeries?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. all right. let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam. on physical exam of your left knee, there is some edema and effusion. moderate range of motion. pain with flexion and extension of the knee. negative lachman\'s. negative valgus and varus stress test.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] all right. let\'s go ahead and take a look at your x-rays. four views of your left knee were taken. these reveal no evidence of any fractures or dislocations. no other abnormalities are noted.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] all right. so, based on your exam and your radiographic findings, it looks like you have a possible mcl strain. i\'m gon na go ahead and put you in a knee immobilizer to provide increased support for the next week. i\'m gon na prescribe you tramadol 50 milligrams, and you can take tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. i\'m gon na have you ice your left knee three to four times a day for twenty to thirty minutes at a time. and i\'m gon na have you refrain from running for the next three weeks. if your pain does not improve with the immobilizer, i\'m gon na have you obtain an mri.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] does that sound good?\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] all right. is there anything else i can help you with today?\n[patient] no.\n[doctor] okay. i\'m gon na go ahead and go ahead and get my nurse to help you get you into the immobilizer. do you have any questions for me?\n[